  "live_records": {
    "q-5144": {
      "dom_id": "step-q5144",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+({y}^{2}-6y) & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =3+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+4)+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+9) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-2,3)$ and its radius is $4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
