      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}+y=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}+y & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}+y) & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}y+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-(-\\frac{1}{2}))}^{2} & ={2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $2.$",
