    "q-76369": {
      "dom_id": "step-q76369",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}-4y=-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{y}^{2}-4y & =-2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =-2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-2)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
